    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nSvelteKit uses parenthesized/bracketed directory names. If I try to `:e` one of these on Windows, tab completion fails because it is interpreting `\\(` as an escaped parenthesis instead of a dir separator. It autocompletes the parenthesized directory, but the parenthesis need to be escaped manually, otherwise it will open the wrong (nonexistent) file. If I escape the name manually and then press tab, it will remove the backslashes, so to edit one of these SvelteKit files I have to type the entire path without autocompletion.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nPowerShell / nvim:\n```\nmkdir \"foo\\(bar)\"\necho test > \"foo\\(bar)\\baz.txt\"\nnvim\n:e foo\\ <press tab>\n:e foo\\\\(bar\\)\\ <press tab>\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nCompletion should automatically escape the parenthesis. It looks like `[]` don't need to be escaped, but it won't autocomplete past a directory with those in the name either.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\n0.12.6\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nNo, vim 9.2.0000. Vim doesn't autocomplete, but does need the ()'s escaped.\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nWindows 11 26H2\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nWindows Terminal 1.25.2733\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nDoesn't apply on Windows\n\n### Installation\n\nwinget",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "@parkovski, this issue also exists in Vim, and there probably won't be a perfect solution in the short term. I'd guess `(` is unlikely to be added to `isfname` — you can set it as needed. As for `[`, the current logic can't distinguish whether you intend to use it as a wildcard or just as a literal character.\nIf needed, you could try a custom command — here's a rough demo from AI. BTW, even on Windows, it's recommended to use `/` as the path separator whenever possible. :)\n```lua\nvim.cmd.cnoreabbrev([[<expr> e (getcmdtype() == ':' && getcmdline() =~ '^e$') ? 'EditFile' : 'e']])\nlocal function complete(lead, _, _)\n  local base = vim.fs.basename(lead) or ''\n  local dir = vim.fs.dirname(lead) or ''\n  local relative = (dir == '' or dir == '.')\n  local out = {}\n  for name, ftype in vim.fs.dir(relative and '.' or dir) do\n    if name:sub(1, #base) == base\n        and not (base:sub(1, 1) ~= '.' and name:sub(1, 1) == '.') then\n      local cand = (relative and '' or dir .. '/') .. name\n      if ftype == 'directory' then\n        cand = cand .. '/'\n      end\n      out[#out + 1] = cand\n    end\n  end\n  table.sort(out)\n  return out\nend\nvim.api.nvim_create_user_command('EditFile', function(opts)\n  local args\n  if opts.args ~= '' then\n    args = { opts.args }\n  end\n  vim.api.nvim_cmd({ cmd = 'edit', bang = opts.bang, args = args }, {})\nend, {\n  nargs = '?',\n  bang = true,\n  complete = complete,\n  desc = ':edit with literal file completion',\n})\n```\n\n<img height=\"140\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/d7c46292-04c1-4a97-b5c4-0a104458fb0c\" />",
